["A student makes sodium chloride by reacting together 0.025 mol of sodium carbonate with an excess of 0.2 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid: Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂. Which statements about the quantities of substance are correct? (1) 600 cm³ of carbon dioxide are produced at room temperature and pressure.  (2) 250 cm³ of the hydrochloric acid are needed to exactly neutralise the sodium carbonate.  (3) 1.46 g of sodium chloride are produced.", "1 and 2 only are correct", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "1 only is correct", "(1) n(CO₂) = 0.025 mol, so V = 0.025 × 24 000 = 600 cm³ ✓. (2) n(HCl) = 2 × 0.025 = 0.050 mol, so V = 0.050 / 0.2 = 0.25 dm³ = 250 cm³ ✓. (3) n(NaCl) = 0.050 mol, so mass = 0.050 × 58.5 = 2.93 g, not 1.46 g ✗."],
["Two hydrocarbons have the formulae C(W)H(X) and C(Y)H(Z), where W, X, Y and Z represent different whole numbers and W/X = Y/Z. Which row is correct when comparing the two hydrocarbons?", "Empirical formula the same, molecular formula different, relative molecular mass different", "Empirical formula different, molecular formula the same, relative molecular mass different", "Empirical formula different, molecular formula the same, relative molecular mass the same", "Empirical formula the same, molecular formula different, relative molecular mass the same", "W/X = Y/Z means the C : H ratio is the same, so the empirical formula is the same. Because W, X, Y and Z are different numbers, the molecular formulae are different, so the relative molecular masses are different too."],
["Sodium hydroxide neutralises acid: H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. In an 11 000 dm³ sample of an aqueous solution, the concentration of acid, [H⁺], is 1.26 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³. Which mass of solid sodium hydroxide neutralises the acid?", "554 g", "0.0214 g", "0.0504 g", "236 g", "n(H⁺) = 11 000 × 1.26 × 10⁻³ = 13.86 mol. The reaction is 1 : 1, so n(NaOH) = 13.86 mol. Mass = 13.86 × 40.0 = 554 g."],
["Compound Q contains 40% carbon by mass. What could Q be? (1) glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆  (2) starch, (C₆H₁₀O₅)ₙ  (3) sucrose, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁", "1 only is correct", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "(1) Glucose: 72.0 / 180.0 = 40.0% C ✓. (2) Starch repeat unit C₆H₁₀O₅: 72.0 / 162.0 = 44.4% ✗. (3) Sucrose: 144.0 / 342.0 = 42.1% ✗. Only glucose fits."],
["Which fuel would produce the largest mass of CO₂ when 10 kg of the fuel undergo complete combustion?", "Octane, C₈H₁₈", "Biodiesel, C₁₇H₃₄O₂", "Ethanol, C₂H₆O", "Propane, C₃H₈", "The mass of CO₂ is proportional to the moles of carbon per gram of fuel: octane 8 / 114 = 0.0702, propane 3 / 44 = 0.0682, biodiesel 17 / 270 = 0.0630 and ethanol 2 / 46 = 0.0435. Octane gives the most CO₂."],
["One mole of sulfuric acid is used to make an aqueous solution. The solution contains H₂SO₄ molecules, H⁺ ions, SO₄²⁻ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions. Which statements are correct? (1) The solution contains 6.02 × 10²³ sulfur atoms.  (2) The solution contains an exactly equal number of H⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions.  (3) One mole of SO₄²⁻ ions contains two moles of electrons.", "1 only is correct", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "(1) Sulfur atoms are conserved: 1 mol of H₂SO₄ contains 1 mol of S = 6.02 × 10²³ atoms ✓. (2) HSO₄⁻ also ionises to H⁺ and SO₄²⁻, so H⁺ ions outnumber HSO₄⁻ ions ✗. (3) SO₄²⁻ has 16 + 4 × 8 + 2 = 50 electrons per ion, so one mole contains 50 mol of electrons ✗."],
["Compound J burns in excess oxygen to give carbon dioxide and water only. When a 3.00 g sample of J is burnt in excess oxygen, 4.40 g of carbon dioxide and 1.80 g of water are formed. What is the empirical formula of J?", "CH₂O", "CH", "CHO", "CH₂", "n(C) = 4.40 / 44.0 = 0.100 mol (1.20 g). n(H) = 2 × 1.80 / 18.0 = 0.200 mol (0.20 g). Mass of O = 3.00 − 1.20 − 0.20 = 1.60 g = 0.100 mol. C : H : O = 1 : 2 : 1, so the empirical formula is CH₂O."],
["For complete combustion, 1 mol of an organic compound X requires 2.5 mol of O₂. Which compounds could be X? (1) C₂H₅OH  (2) C₂H₂  (3) CH₃CHO", "2 and 3 only are correct", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "1 only is correct", "(1) C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O needs 3 mol ✗. (2) C₂H₂ + 2½O₂ → 2CO₂ + H₂O needs 2.5 mol ✓. (3) CH₃CHO + 2½O₂ → 2CO₂ + 2H₂O needs 2.5 mol ✓."],
["Three samples of chlorine gas each contain 0.710 g of chlorine. In the first reaction a sample is reacted completely with hydrogen gas. In the second a sample is reacted completely with cold NaOH(aq). In the third a sample is reacted completely with hot NaOH(aq). Which masses of the named products would be formed? (1) Exactly 0.730 g of HCl form in the first reaction.  (2) Exactly 0.585 g of NaCl form in the second reaction.  (3) Exactly 0.975 g of NaCl form in the third reaction.", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "1 only is correct", "n(Cl₂) = 0.710 / 71.0 = 0.0100 mol. (1) Cl₂ + H₂ → 2HCl: 0.0200 mol × 36.5 = 0.730 g ✓. (2) Cold NaOH: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl + H₂O, so 0.0100 mol NaCl = 0.585 g ✓. (3) Hot NaOH: 3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O, so 0.0100 × 5/3 = 0.0167 mol NaCl = 0.975 g ✓."],
["Aspirin, C₉H₈O₄ (Mr = 180.0), can be made by reacting 2-hydroxybenzoic acid, C₇H₆O₃ (Mr = 138.0), with ethanoic anhydride, C₄H₆O₃ (Mr = 102.0): C₇H₆O₃ + C₄H₆O₃ → C₉H₈O₄ + C₂H₄O₂. If a reaction mixture consists of 10.0 g of each of the two reactants, what is the maximum mass of aspirin that can be produced?", "13.0 g", "5.7 g", "10.0 g", "17.6 g", "n(C₇H₆O₃) = 10.0 / 138.0 = 0.0725 mol and n(C₄H₆O₃) = 10.0 / 102.0 = 0.0980 mol. The reaction is 1 : 1, so 2-hydroxybenzoic acid is limiting. Maximum aspirin = 0.0725 × 180.0 = 13.0 g."],
["For a fixed mass of an ideal gas, which graph is a straight line through the origin?", "V against T (in kelvin) at constant p", "p against V at constant T", "pV against p at constant T", "pV against V at constant T", "From pV = nRT, at constant p the volume V is directly proportional to T in kelvin, which gives a straight line through the origin. At constant T the product pV is constant (a horizontal line), and p is inversely proportional to V (a curve)."],
["Complete combustion of a sample of a hydrocarbon gave 0.132 g of carbon dioxide and 0.054 g of water. Which formulae could correctly represent this hydrocarbon? (1) CH₂=CH₂  (2) CH₃CH=CH₂  (3) CH₃CH=CHCH₃", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "1 only is correct", "n(C) = 0.132 / 44.0 = 0.00300 mol and n(H) = 2 × 0.054 / 18.0 = 0.00600 mol. C : H = 1 : 2, so the empirical formula is CH₂. Ethene, propene and but-2-ene all have this ratio."],
["Under room conditions, 600 cm³ of a gas, X, has a mass of 0.700 g. What could X be? (1) carbon monoxide  (2) ethene  (3) nitrogen", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "1 only is correct", "n = 600 / 24 000 = 0.0250 mol, so Mr = 0.700 / 0.0250 = 28.0. CO (28.0), C₂H₄ (28.0) and N₂ (28.0) all fit."],
["A sample of the hydrocarbon C₆H₁₂ is completely burned in dry oxygen. The product gases pass first through tube P (anhydrous calcium chloride, which absorbs H₂O) and then through tube Q (soda lime, which absorbs CO₂). What is the ratio of the mass increase of P to the mass increase of Q?", "0.41", "0.82", "1.2", "2.4", "C₆H₁₂ + 9O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O. For 1 mol of C₆H₁₂, P gains 6 × 18.0 = 108 g and Q gains 6 × 44.0 = 264 g. Ratio = 108 / 264 = 0.41."],
["5.0 g samples of the carbonates of barium, copper, lithium and magnesium are decomposed to the metal oxides and carbon dioxide. For which compound is there the greatest loss in mass?", "Lithium carbonate", "Barium carbonate", "Copper(II) carbonate", "Magnesium carbonate", "Each mole of carbonate loses 1 mol of CO₂ (44.0 g), so the loss from 5.0 g is 5.0 × 44.0 / Mr and the smallest Mr loses most. Mr: Li₂CO₃ 73.8, MgCO₃ 84.3, CuCO₃ 123.5, BaCO₃ 197.3. Li₂CO₃ loses about 3.0 g."],
["Beams of charged particles are deflected by an electrical field. The angle of deflection of a particle is proportional to its charge / mass ratio. In an experiment, protons are deflected by an angle of +15°. In another experiment under identical conditions, ²H⁻ ions are deflected by an angle of Y°. What is the value of Y?", "−7.5", "−30.0", "+7.5", "+30.0", "A proton has charge +1 and mass 1, so charge / mass = +1. A ²H⁻ ion has charge −1 and mass 2, so charge / mass = −½. Y = 15 × (−½) = −7.5."],
["Zinc reacts with hydrochloric acid: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. Which statements are correct? (1) A 3.27 g sample of zinc reacts with an excess of hydrochloric acid to give 0.0500 mol of zinc chloride.  (2) A 6.54 g sample of zinc reacts completely with exactly 100 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid.  (3) A 13.08 g sample of zinc reacts with an excess of hydrochloric acid to give 9.60 dm³ of hydrogen, measured at room conditions.", "1 only is correct", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "(1) n(Zn) = 3.27 / 65.4 = 0.0500 mol, giving 0.0500 mol ZnCl₂ ✓. (2) 6.54 g Zn = 0.100 mol, which needs 0.200 mol HCl = 200 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³ acid, not 100 cm³ ✗. (3) 13.08 g Zn = 0.200 mol, giving 0.200 × 24 = 4.80 dm³ of H₂, not 9.60 dm³ ✗."],
["A 3.7 g sample of copper(II) carbonate is added to 25 cm³ of 2.0 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid. Which volume of gas is produced under room conditions?", "0.60 dm³", "0.72 dm³", "1.20 dm³", "2.40 dm³", "n(CuCO₃) = 3.7 / 123.5 = 0.030 mol and n(HCl) = 0.025 × 2.0 = 0.050 mol. CuCO₃ + 2HCl → CuCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂ needs 0.060 mol HCl to react all the CuCO₃, so HCl is limiting. n(CO₂) = 0.050 / 2 = 0.025 mol, and V = 0.025 × 24 = 0.60 dm³."],
["A washing powder contains sodium hydrogencarbonate, NaHCO₃, as one of the ingredients. In a titration, a solution containing 1.00 g of this washing powder requires 7.15 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sulfuric acid for complete reaction. The sodium hydrogencarbonate is the only ingredient that reacts with the acid. What is the percentage by mass of sodium hydrogencarbonate in the washing powder?", "12.0%", "3.0%", "6.0%", "24.0%", "n(H₂SO₄) = 7.15 × 10⁻³ × 0.100 = 7.15 × 10⁻⁴ mol. 2NaHCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O + 2CO₂, so n(NaHCO₃) = 1.43 × 10⁻³ mol. Mass = 1.43 × 10⁻³ × 84.0 = 0.120 g, which is 12.0% of 1.00 g."],
["Diamond is a pure form of carbon. The mass of a diamond can be measured in carats. One carat is 0.200 g of carbon. Which expression gives the number of carats that contain 6.02 × 10²³ carbon atoms?", "12.0 ÷ 0.200", "0.200 × 12.0", "0.200 ÷ 12.0", "(0.200 ÷ 6.02 × 10²³) × 12.0", "6.02 × 10²³ atoms is 1.00 mol of carbon, which has a mass of 12.0 g. Each carat is 0.200 g, so the number of carats = 12.0 / 0.200."],
["In this question assume that (NH₄)₂CO₃·H₂O(s) dissolves in water without causing an increase in volume. Which mass of (NH₄)₂CO₃·H₂O(s) should be added to 800 cm³ of water to form a 0.100 mol dm⁻³ solution of NH₄⁺ ions?", "4.56 g", "7.13 g", "9.12 g", "14.3 g", "n(NH₄⁺) = 0.800 × 0.100 = 0.0800 mol. Each formula unit gives 2 NH₄⁺, so n(salt) = 0.0400 mol. Mr = 2(18.0) + 12.0 + 48.0 + 18.0 = 114.0, so mass = 0.0400 × 114.0 = 4.56 g."],
["Sodium azide, NaN₃, decomposes: 2NaN₃ → 2Na + 3N₂. Which volume of nitrogen, measured at room temperature and pressure, will be produced by the decomposition of 150 g of sodium azide?", "83 dm³", "166 dm³", "55 dm³", "37 dm³", "Mr(NaN₃) = 65.0, so n = 150 / 65.0 = 2.31 mol. 2NaN₃ → 3N₂, so n(N₂) = 2.31 × 3/2 = 3.46 mol. V = 3.46 × 24 = 83 dm³."],
["A sample of solid ammonium chloride decomposes on heating: solid ammonium chloride → ammonia gas + hydrogen chloride gas. A total of 2.4 × 10²¹ molecules of gas is formed. How many hydrogen atoms are present in the gaseous products?", "4.8 × 10²¹", "1.2 × 10²¹", "2.4 × 10²¹", "9.6 × 10²¹", "NH₄Cl → NH₃ + HCl gives equal numbers of the two gases: 1.2 × 10²¹ NH₃ and 1.2 × 10²¹ HCl molecules. H atoms = 3 × 1.2 × 10²¹ + 1 × 1.2 × 10²¹ = 4.8 × 10²¹."],
["A copper ore contains 3.00% of copper carbonate, CuCO₃, by mass. Which mass of copper would be obtained from 1 tonne of the ore?", "15.4 kg", "1.91 kg", "3.71 kg", "58.4 kg", "1 tonne = 1000 kg, so the ore contains 30.0 kg of CuCO₃. Mr(CuCO₃) = 123.5, so the fraction of Cu = 63.5 / 123.5 = 0.514. Mass of Cu = 30.0 × 0.514 = 15.4 kg."],
["In this question you should assume air contains 21% oxygen. What is the minimum volume of air required to ensure complete combustion of 10 cm³ of butane gas, under room conditions?", "310 cm³", "14 cm³", "27 cm³", "65 cm³", "C₄H₁₀ + 6½O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O. 10 cm³ of butane needs 65 cm³ of O₂. Air is 21% oxygen, so the volume of air = 65 / 0.21 = 310 cm³."],
["Strontium metal can be extracted from strontium oxide, SrO, by reduction with aluminium: 6SrO + 2Al → 3Sr + Sr₃Al₂O₆. What is the maximum mass of strontium metal that can be produced from the reduction of 100 g of strontium oxide using this reaction?", "42.3 g", "41.3 g", "84.6 g", "169.2 g", "Mr(SrO) = 103.6, so n(SrO) = 100 / 103.6 = 0.965 mol. 6SrO gives 3Sr, so n(Sr) = 0.965 × 3/6 = 0.483 mol. Mass = 0.483 × 87.6 = 42.3 g."],
["An ore of manganese contains 4% by mass of MnO₂ and no other manganese compound. Which mass of manganese would be obtained from 1 tonne of this ore?", "25.3 kg", "40.0 kg", "63.3 kg", "632 kg", "1 tonne = 1000 kg, so there is 40 kg of MnO₂. Mr(MnO₂) = 86.9, so the fraction of Mn = 54.9 / 86.9 = 0.632. Mass of Mn = 40 × 0.632 = 25.3 kg."],
["Substance Q is a hydrocarbon. When 1.00 g of Q is completely burned, 3.22 g of carbon dioxide is produced. What could be the identity of Q?", "Cyclohexene", "Cyclopentane", "Ethene", "Pentane", "Mass of C = 3.22 × 12.0 / 44.0 = 0.878 g, so mass of H = 0.122 g. n(C) = 0.0732 mol and n(H) = 0.122 mol, so C : H = 1 : 1.67 = 3 : 5. Cyclohexene (C₆H₁₀) fits. Ethene and cyclopentane are CH₂ (1 : 2) and pentane (C₅H₁₂) is 1 : 2.4."],
["Originally, chemists thought indium oxide had the formula InO. By experiment they showed that 4.8 g of indium combined with 1.0 g of oxygen to produce 5.8 g of indium oxide. The Ar of oxygen was known to be 16. Which value for the Ar of indium is calculated using these data and the original formula InO?", "77", "38", "115", "154", "With the formula InO, n(In) = n(O) = 1.0 / 16 = 0.0625 mol, so Ar(In) = 4.8 / 0.0625 = 76.8, which is 77. (The true formula is In₂O₃, which would give about 115.)"],
["Which contains the largest number of hydrogen atoms?", "1.00 mol of hydrogen molecules", "0.10 mol of pentane", "0.20 mol of but-2-ene", "6.02 × 10²³ hydrogen atoms", "Moles of H atoms: 0.10 mol C₅H₁₂ → 1.2 mol; 0.20 mol C₄H₈ → 1.6 mol; 1.00 mol H₂ → 2.0 mol; 6.02 × 10²³ H atoms → 1.0 mol. Hydrogen molecules give the most."],
["What is the minimum mass of oxygen required to ensure the complete combustion of 12 dm³ of propane measured under room conditions?", "80 g", "60 g", "120 g", "160 g", "n(C₃H₈) = 12 / 24 = 0.50 mol. C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O, so n(O₂) = 2.5 mol. Mass = 2.5 × 32.0 = 80 g."],
["Ethanol combines with ethanoic acid to form ethyl ethanoate: C₂H₅OH(l) + CH₃CO₂H(l) ⇌ CH₃CO₂C₂H₅(l) + H₂O(l), ΔH° = −6 kJ mol⁻¹. 9.2 g ethanol, 12 g ethanoic acid and 8.8 g ethyl ethanoate are mixed and allowed to stand at 298 K until equilibrium is reached (Mr: C₂H₅OH 46; CH₃CO₂H 60; CH₃CO₂C₂H₅ 88). The equilibrium mixture contains 4.8 g of ethanoic acid. The experiment is repeated at 323 K. Which statements are correct? (1) There are 0.22 moles of ethyl ethanoate in the mixture at equilibrium at 298 K.  (2) The equilibrium mixture at 323 K will contain more than 4.8 g of ethanoic acid.  (3) If a small amount of water is added at the start of either experiment, the value of Kc would not be affected.", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "1 only is correct", "(1) Ethanoic acid falls from 0.20 mol to 0.080 mol (4.8 g), so 0.12 mol reacted and ester = 0.10 + 0.12 = 0.22 mol ✓. (2) The forward reaction is exothermic, so raising the temperature shifts the equilibrium left and more ethanoic acid remains ✓. (3) Kc depends only on temperature, so adding water does not change it ✓."],
["A gaseous hydrocarbon has a density of 2.42 g dm⁻³ under room conditions. What could the hydrocarbon be? (1) butane, C₄H₁₀  (2) methylcyclopropane, C₄H₈  (3) butene, C₄H₈", "1 only is correct", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "Mr = density × molar volume = 2.42 × 24 = 58.1. Butane (C₄H₁₀) has Mr = 58.0 ✓. Methylcyclopropane and butene are both C₄H₈ with Mr = 56.0 ✗."],
["Which statement about the Avogadro constant is correct?", "It is the number of atoms in one mole of neon.", "It is the mass of one mole of any element.", "It is the mass of 6.02 × 10²³ atoms of any element.", "It is the number of atoms in 12 g of any element.", "The Avogadro constant (6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹) is the number of particles in one mole. Neon is monatomic, so one mole of neon contains that many atoms. The mass of a mole differs between elements, and 12 g is one mole only for carbon-12."],
["Compound X is a straight chain hydrocarbon with an Mr of 84. What can be determined about X? (1) empirical formula  (2) molecular formula  (3) whether X contains a C=C bond or not", "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "1 and 2 only are correct", "2 and 3 only are correct", "1 only is correct", "Mr = 84 fits only C₆H₁₂ (6 × 12 + 12), so the molecular formula is C₆H₁₂ and the empirical formula is CH₂. A straight chain C₆H₁₂ has two fewer H atoms than hexane (C₆H₁₄), so it must contain one C=C bond. All three can be determined."],
["Compound X is an organic compound that contains 30.6% carbon, 3.8% hydrogen, 20.4% oxygen and 45.2% chlorine by mass. What is the empirical formula of X?", "C₂H₃OCl", "C₂H₄OCl", "C₃H₄OCl", "C₄H₃O₂Cl₂", "Moles per 100 g: C 30.6 / 12.0 = 2.55; H 3.8 / 1.0 = 3.8; O 20.4 / 16.0 = 1.275; Cl 45.2 / 35.5 = 1.273. Dividing by 1.273 gives C : H : O : Cl = 2 : 3 : 1 : 1."],
["A sample of propane, C₃H₈, with a mass of 9.61 g is completely combusted in an excess of oxygen under room conditions. Which volume of carbon dioxide gas is produced?", "15.7 dm³", "4.89 dm³", "5.24 dm³", "14.7 dm³", "n(C₃H₈) = 9.61 / 44.0 = 0.218 mol. C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O, so n(CO₂) = 3 × 0.218 = 0.655 mol. V = 0.655 × 24 = 15.7 dm³."],
["Copper dissolves in dilute nitric acid producing a blue solution of Cu(NO₃)₂, water and nitrogen(II) oxide as the only products. How many moles of acid react with three moles of copper in the balanced equation?", "8", "2", "4", "6", "3Cu + 8HNO₃ → 3Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO + 4H₂O. Three moles of copper react with 8 moles of nitric acid."],
["2.0 g of ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃, decomposes to give 0.90 g of water and a single gas. What is the identity of the gas?", "N₂O", "NO", "NO₂", "N₂", "n(NH₄NO₃) = 2.0 / 80.0 = 0.025 mol and n(H₂O) = 0.90 / 18.0 = 0.050 mol, so each NH₄NO₃ gives 2 H₂O. NH₄NO₃ → 2H₂O + N₂O, so the gas is N₂O."],
["Compound X consists of 40.0% carbon, 6.7% hydrogen and 53.3% oxygen by mass. What is the empirical formula of compound X?", "CH₂O", "C₂H₂O", "C₂H₄O", "CHO", "Moles per 100 g: C 40.0 / 12.0 = 3.33; H 6.7 / 1.0 = 6.7; O 53.3 / 16.0 = 3.33. The ratio C : H : O = 1 : 2 : 1."],
["Which statement is correct?", "16 g of methane gas contains 3.0 × 10²⁴ atoms.", "1.0 g of hydrogen gas contains 3.0 × 10²³ atoms.", "4.0 g of helium gas contains 1.2 × 10²⁴ atoms.", "44 g of carbon dioxide gas contains 6.0 × 10²³ atoms.", "16 g of CH₄ is 1.0 mol of molecules, each with 5 atoms, so 5.0 mol of atoms = 3.0 × 10²⁴ ✓. 1.0 g of H₂ is 0.5 mol of molecules = 1.0 mol of atoms = 6.0 × 10²³ ✗. 4.0 g of He is 1.0 mol = 6.0 × 10²³ atoms ✗. 44 g of CO₂ is 1.0 mol of molecules with 3 atoms each = 1.8 × 10²⁴ atoms ✗."],
["Compound X contains the elements C, H and O only. 2.00 g of X produces 4.00 g of carbon dioxide and 1.63 g of water when completely combusted. What is the empirical formula of X?", "C₂H₄O", "CHO₂", "C₂H₂O", "CH₂O₂", "n(C) = 4.00 / 44.0 = 0.0909 mol (1.09 g). n(H) = 2 × 1.63 / 18.0 = 0.181 mol (0.18 g). Mass of O = 2.00 − 1.09 − 0.18 = 0.73 g = 0.0455 mol. C : H : O = 2 : 4 : 1."],
["Which statement about atoms and molecules is correct?", "The relative formula mass of a compound is the sum of the individual atomic masses of all the atoms in the formula.", "The molecular formula of a compound is the simplest whole number ratio of atoms of each element in the compound.", "One mole of any substance contains 6 × 10²³ atoms.", "The relative atomic mass of an element is the ratio of the average mass of one atom of the element to the mass of an atom of carbon-12.", "The relative formula mass is found by adding the atomic masses of all the atoms in the formula. The simplest ratio describes the empirical formula, not the molecular formula. A mole of a substance contains 6 × 10²³ particles, which are not always atoms. Relative atomic mass compares the average mass of an atom with 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom, not the whole atom."],
["In separate experiments, 5.0 g samples of each of four s-block metals are added to an excess of water. The gas evolved is collected and its volume measured under the same conditions of temperature and pressure for each sample. Which metal produces the largest volume of gas?", "Calcium", "Potassium", "Rubidium", "Strontium", "Ca and Sr give 1 mol of H₂ per mole of metal, but K and Rb give only ½ mol. Moles of H₂ from 5.0 g: Ca 5.0 / 40.1 = 0.125; K ½ × 5.0 / 39.1 = 0.064; Rb ½ × 5.0 / 85.5 = 0.029; Sr 5.0 / 87.6 = 0.057. Calcium gives the most."],
["A student reacts 1 mol of copper with concentrated nitric acid to produce 1 mol of copper(II) nitrate, 2 mol of water and substance X. No other product is formed. Substance X does not contain copper or hydrogen. What could be substance X?", "NO₂", "N₂", "N₂O", "NO", "2 mol of H₂O contains 4 mol of H, so 4 mol of HNO₃ react (4 N, 12 O). Cu(NO₃)₂ takes 2 N and 6 O and the water takes 2 O, leaving 2 N and 4 O: 2NO₂. Cu + 4HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2H₂O + 2NO₂."],
["Which sample contains the most iodine?", "1 g of NH₄I", "1 g of CaI₂", "1 g of KI", "1 g of NaI", "The mass of iodine in 1 g is the fraction of iodine in the formula: NH₄I 126.9 / 144.9 = 0.876; CaI₂ 253.8 / 293.9 = 0.864; NaI 126.9 / 149.9 = 0.847; KI 126.9 / 166.0 = 0.764. NH₄I has the highest fraction."],
["When a small sample of hydrocarbon Q is completely combusted, it produces 3.52 g of carbon dioxide and 1.44 g of water. What could be the identity of hydrocarbon Q?", "Cyclooctane, C₈H₁₆", "Benzene, C₆H₆", "Buta-1,3-diyne, H–C≡C–C≡C–H", "Cyclohexene, C₆H₁₀", "n(C) = 3.52 / 44.0 = 0.0800 mol and n(H) = 2 × 1.44 / 18.0 = 0.160 mol, so C : H = 1 : 2 and the empirical formula is CH₂. Cyclooctane (C₈H₁₆) fits. Benzene is 1 : 1, buta-1,3-diyne is 2 : 1 and cyclohexene is 3 : 5."],
["Analysis of the hormone thyroxine gives these results. Heating 0.500 g of thyroxine with aqueous silver nitrate produces 0.604 g of silver iodide (all of the iodine is converted to AgI). Complete combustion of 0.500 g of thyroxine produces 232 cm³ of carbon dioxide and 7.72 cm³ of nitrogen, measured under room conditions. Which molecular formula of thyroxine agrees with these values?", "C₁₅H₁₁NO₄I₄ (Mr = 776.6)", "C₁₅H₇NO₄I₈ (Mr = 1280.2)", "C₃₀H₂₅NO₆I₄ (Mr = 1002.6)", "C₃₀H₂₁NO₆I₈ (Mr = 1506.2)", "n(I) = n(AgI) = 0.604 / 234.8 = 2.57 × 10⁻³ mol. n(C) = n(CO₂) = 0.232 / 24 = 9.67 × 10⁻³ mol. n(N) = 2 × 0.00772 / 24 = 6.43 × 10⁻⁴ mol. C : N : I = 15 : 1 : 4. Only C₁₅H₁₁NO₄I₄ matches, and 0.500 g / 776.6 = 6.4 × 10⁻⁴ mol agrees with 1 N per molecule."],
["How many moles of oxygen, O₂, are needed to burn 1 mol of ethane if the products of the reaction are water and carbon only?", "1.5", "3", "3.5", "5", "C₂H₆ + 1½O₂ → 2C + 3H₂O. Oxygen is needed only to form water: 3 mol of H₂O needs 3 mol of O atoms = 1.5 mol of O₂."],
["Which sample contains the same number of the named species as the number of molecules in 35.5 g of chlorine?", "Atoms in 16 g of sulfur", "Atoms in 23 g of sodium", "Ions in 74.5 g of potassium chloride", "Molecules in 88 g of carbon dioxide", "35.5 g of Cl₂ is 35.5 / 71.0 = 0.500 mol of molecules. 16 g of S is 16 / 32.1 = 0.50 mol of atoms ✓. 23 g of Na is 1.0 mol of atoms; 74.5 g of KCl is 1.0 mol of KCl = 2.0 mol of ions; 88 g of CO₂ is 2.0 mol of molecules."],
["Mixture R consists of one mole of C₃H₆ and one mole of C₄H₆. What is the minimum number of moles of oxygen molecules needed for complete combustion of mixture R?", "10", "6.5", "7", "20", "C₃H₆ + 4½O₂ → 3CO₂ + 3H₂O and C₄H₆ + 5½O₂ → 4CO₂ + 3H₂O. Total O₂ = 4.5 + 5.5 = 10 mol."],
["Sodium peroxide, Na₂O₂, is used to absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and release oxygen in closed environments such as space capsules and submarines: 2Na₂O₂ + 2CO₂ → 2Na₂CO₃ + O₂. Which mass of sodium peroxide would be required to remove 2.4 dm³ of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere at room temperature and pressure?", "7.8 g", "2.4 g", "3.9 g", "15.6 g", "n(CO₂) = 2.4 / 24 = 0.10 mol. The ratio Na₂O₂ : CO₂ is 1 : 1, so n(Na₂O₂) = 0.10 mol. Mr = 2(23.0) + 2(16.0) = 78.0, so mass = 7.8 g."],
["Calcium oxide and magnesium sulfide each react with acid: CaO(s) + 2H⁺(aq) → Ca²⁺(aq) + H₂O(l) and MgS(s) + 2H⁺(aq) → Mg²⁺(aq) + H₂S(g). A mixture of these two compounds, X, reacts with exactly 0.125 mol of dilute hydrochloric acid. The amount of hydrogen sulfide formed is 0.0250 mol. What was the mass of calcium oxide in mixture X?", "2.1 g", "1.4 g", "2.8 g", "4.2 g", "n(MgS) = n(H₂S) = 0.0250 mol, which uses 2 × 0.0250 = 0.0500 mol of H⁺. The remaining H⁺ = 0.125 − 0.0500 = 0.0750 mol reacts with CaO (2 H⁺ each), so n(CaO) = 0.0375 mol. Mass = 0.0375 × 56.1 = 2.1 g."],
["Molten aluminium chloride has a simple molecular structure. Each molecule consists of two aluminium atoms and six chlorine atoms. Which statement is correct?", "One mole of molten aluminium chloride contains 3.61 × 10²⁴ chlorine atoms.", "All the chlorine atoms in 1 g of molten aluminium chloride have the same mass.", "One mole of molten aluminium chloride contains 6.02 × 10²³ aluminium atoms.", "The empirical formula of molten aluminium chloride is Al₂Cl₆.", "One mole of Al₂Cl₆ molecules contains 6 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.61 × 10²⁴ Cl atoms ✓. Chlorine has isotopes (³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl) so its atoms do not all have the same mass. One mole contains 1.2 × 10²⁴ Al atoms. The empirical formula is AlCl₃."],
["Hydrated cobalt(II) sulfate loses water when heated to give anhydrous cobalt(II) sulfate. All the water of crystallisation is lost to the atmosphere as steam. When 3.10 g of hydrated cobalt(II) sulfate, CoSO₄·xH₂O, is heated to constant mass, the loss in mass is 1.39 g. What is the value of x, to the nearest whole number?", "7", "4", "6", "11", "Mass of anhydrous CoSO₄ = 3.10 − 1.39 = 1.71 g. Mr(CoSO₄) = 58.9 + 32.1 + 64.0 = 155.0, so n = 1.71 / 155.0 = 0.0110 mol. n(H₂O) = 1.39 / 18.0 = 0.0772 mol. Ratio = 0.0772 / 0.0110 = 7.0, so x = 7."],
["Which statement is correct?", "The relative formula mass of CaCO₃ is 100.1.", "The relative atomic mass of a ³⁵Cl atom is 35.5.", "The relative isotopic mass of a ²⁴Mg atom is 24.3.", "The relative molecular mass of O₂ is 16.0.", "Mr(CaCO₃) = 40.1 + 12.0 + 3(16.0) = 100.1 ✓. 35.5 is the relative atomic mass of the element chlorine (an average); a ³⁵Cl atom has relative isotopic mass 35. A ²⁴Mg atom has relative isotopic mass 24.0 (24.3 is the Ar of magnesium). Mr(O₂) = 32.0."]
